@startuml 04-auth-flow
' Source of truth for docs/images/04-auth-flow.svg — regenerated by
' .github/workflows/render-diagrams.yml. Edit this file, never the SVG.
'
' A sequence diagram is a deliberate choice: authentication here is less about
' structure (who is composed of what) and more about a STRICTLY TIME-ORDERED
' exchange of messages between participants (browser, Edge proxy, Route
' Handler, auth service, database) — exactly what a sequence diagram exists
' for. A state diagram would fit if we only cared about which state a session
' is in by itself (alive/expired/revoked) — that is one narrow slice of the
' behavior, not the whole of it, hence not chosen here. An activity diagram
' would fit a single scenario end-to-end without caring who initiates each
' step — but for authentication, "who sent what to whom, in what order" is
' the whole point, and that is a question about roles, not only about logic.
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam DefaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam SequenceArrowColor #33475B
skinparam SequenceLifeLineBorderColor #9AA5B1
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #33475B
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FBEFD5
skinparam NoteBorderColor #A9822C

actor "Browser" as Browser
participant "proxy.ts\n(Edge: rate-limit only)" as Proxy
participant "Route Handler\n/api/auth/*" as Route
participant "auth-service.ts" as Service
participant "password.ts\n(Argon2id)" as Password
database "Postgres\n(User, Session)" as DB

== Login ==
Browser -> Proxy : POST /api/auth/login\n{email, password}
Proxy -> Route : passes through\n(IP rate-limit cleared)
Route -> Route : checkLoginRateLimit\nlogin:ip:* AND login:account:*\n(5 attempts / 15 minutes)
alt limit exceeded
  Route --> Browser : 429 + Retry-After
else within limit
  Route -> Service : login(email, password, {ip, ua})
  Service -> DB : findUnique User by email
  alt user not found
    Service -> Password : verify(dummyHash, password)\n— an artificial delay so the response\ntime can't leak whether\nthe account exists
  else user found
    Service -> Password : verify(user.passwordHash, password)
  end
  alt wrong password or email
    Service --> Route : null
    Route --> Browser : 401 "Invalid email or password."
  else success
    Service -> DB : create Session\n(SHA-256 hash of the opaque\nrefresh token, expiresAt +30d)
    Service -> Service : signAccessToken\n(JWT HS256, 15 minutes)
    Service --> Route : {user, accessToken, refreshToken}
    Route -> Route : reset login:ip:*\nand login:account:*
    Route --> Browser : Set-Cookie access_token,\nrefresh_token (httpOnly,\nSameSite=Strict, Secure in prod)
  end
end

== Validating a request to /api/admin/* ==
Browser -> Proxy : request + access_token cookie
Proxy -> Proxy : IP rate-limit\n(admin-mutation: 120/60s) —\nthis is NOT an identity check
Proxy -> Route : passes through
Route -> Route : defineAdminRoute →\nresolvePrincipal(cookie|Bearer)\n→ verifyAccessToken (jose, no DB hit)
alt JWT valid and has scope admin:*
  Route --> Browser : 200 + data
else JWT invalid/expired
  Route --> Browser : 401 Unauthorized
end

== Silent session refresh ==
Browser -> Route : (after a 401 OR on\nSessionKeepAlive's ~10 min timer)\nPOST /api/auth/refresh
Route -> Service : refreshSession(refreshToken)
Service -> DB : find the Session by\nSHA-256(refreshToken),\nnot revoked, not expired
alt found
  Service -> DB : revokedAt = now()\n(old session — rotation)
  Service -> DB : create a new Session\n(new opaque token)
  Service -> Service : new access JWT
  Service --> Route : new tokens
  Route --> Browser : new Set-Cookie,\noriginal request retried once
else not found / expired / revoked
  Route --> Browser : 401 + cookies cleared\n→ redirect to /admin/login?from=...
end

== Logout ==
Browser -> Route : POST /api/auth/logout
Route -> Service : revokeSession(refreshToken)
Service -> DB : revokedAt = now()
Route --> Browser : cookies cleared\n(works even if the access\nJWT already expired)

note over Service, DB
  Access token — a JWT (verified without a DB round-trip, Edge-friendly).
  Refresh token — NOT a JWT, but 32 random bytes; only its SHA-256 hash
  is stored, so a leaked database dump alone can't be used to impersonate
  a session. Rotation on every refresh is a signal for future detection
  of a replayed (reused) token.
end note

@enduml
